**Temple of the Forge**

The Temple of the Forge sits on the lower slope of the Temple District, closer to the Marketplace than most of its neighbors. You hear it before you see it: the steady ring of hammers, the low roar of the main hearth, and the occasional sharp hiss of quenching metal.

The building is broad and practical. Dark stone walls, soot-stained around the high vents, a wide open front that can be closed with iron-bound shutters at night. There is no grand idol in the courtyard. Instead a single massive anvil stands under the open sky, its surface scarred by generations of ceremonial and working strikes. Offerings left on it are usually small tools, broken blades, or a pinch of good ore.

Inside, the temple is a working forge first and a place of worship second. Multiple hearths run along the long walls. Smiths and artisans work in the open, some wearing simple iron tokens of the Forge, others simply here because this is where the best heat and the best company are found. The air is hot, dry, and metallic. Sparks drift up into the high rafters and die.

### What the party can get here

**Basic & repair work**  
- Sharpening, mending, re-hafting, simple modifications — priced in silver, done the same day if the queue is not overloaded.  
- Ordinary weapons and armor (no mechanical tier bonus) available off the rack or made to order in a few days.

**Tier 1 gear (the real draw)**  
This is one of the first places in the capital where a party can actually buy proper mechanical equipment. Stock is limited and not always on display.

Typical offerings (examples, adjust as needed):

| Item                      | Type   | Effect                          | Price    |
|---------------------------|--------|---------------------------------|----------|
| Forge-Touched Blade       | Weapon | +1 attack damage                | 200 gold |
| Quench-Hardened Jacket    | Armor  | Armour 1                        | 200 gold |
| Spark-Guard Gauntlets     | Armor  | Start combat with 1 Resist      | 180 gold |
| Temper-Line Short Sword   | Weapon | Inflict Weak on first successful attack each combat | 200 gold |

Higher-tier work exists but is commission-only and measured in weeks, not days. The temple does not keep Tier 2 or 3 pieces sitting on shelves.

### Key faces

- **The Duty Smith** (rotating) — whoever is currently tending the public counter. Practical, soot-streaked, short on small talk.  
- **Master Harran** — older, heavy-shouldered, speaks for the temple when something more than a simple purchase is required. He decides whether a commission is accepted.  
- A handful of independent artisans who rent hearth space and sell their own work under the temple’s roof. Quality varies; the best of them are worth seeking out.

### Atmosphere & rules of the place
- Loud, hot, and honest.  
- Payment is expected in coin or good metal. Favors are sometimes accepted, but only by the senior smiths.  
- Open blasphemy against craftsmanship is the one thing that reliably gets someone thrown out.  
- The temple does not care what you do with the gear once it leaves. It cares that the work itself was done properly.

This is the natural first stop for a party that has coin and needs real equipment.

**Independent Artisans — Temple of the Forge**

These three rent hearth space under the temple roof. They are not clergy. They pay for the heat, the anvil time, and the right to sell under the Forge’s name. Quality and temperament vary.

---

### 1. Brannok “Sparks”  
**Specialty:** Fast, reliable blades and tools. Nothing fancy.  

A lean man in his thirties with burn scars up both forearms and a permanent squint from the forge light. He works quickly and talks while he works. His pieces are clean, well-balanced, and deliberately unadorned. He keeps a small rack of finished short blades and utility knives for immediate sale.

- **Attitude:** Friendly, slightly manic, always trying to upsell a better edge or a spare knife.  
- **Strength:** Speed and consistency. If you need something serviceable *today*, he is the one.  
- **Weakness:** He does not do armor and refuses commissions that require “art.”  
- **Typical stock:** Ordinary weapons (no tier), plus the occasional Tier 1 short blade (+1 damage) when he has the metal for it.

---

### 2. Sister Calda  
**Specialty:** Armor that prioritizes survival over appearance.  

A broad, quiet woman who once worked the city walls under Cedric’s people and still carries herself like it. She left the formal ranks but never stopped making gear that keeps people standing. Her work is heavier than it needs to be and finished in dark, matte tones. She rarely looks up from the anvil until the piece is done.

- **Attitude:** Curt. Answers questions about the work. Ignores questions about herself.  
- **Strength:** Excellent defensive pieces. She understands what actually fails in a fight.  
- **Weakness:** Slow. A full set takes time and she will not be rushed.  
- **Typical stock:** Tier 1 armor (Armour 1 or start-of-combat Resist). Occasionally a piece with both if the buyer is patient and pays up front.

---

### 3. Vesper  
**Specialty:** Light, precise work — rapiers, balanced sidearms, and small metal components for more complex gear.  

Younger than the other two, pale from spending more time at the finishing bench than the main hearth. Vesper treats metal like it has opinions and listens carefully. Their pieces are elegant without being fragile. They keep a locked cabinet of finished work and only open it for people who ask the right questions.

- **Attitude:** Soft-spoken, observant, slightly amused by most customers.  
- **Strength:** Precision and balance. Best choice for anyone who fights with speed or technique rather than bulk.  
- **Weakness:** Limited volume. They make fewer pieces and charge accordingly.  
- **Typical stock:** Tier 1 finesse weapons (often with a conditional effect such as Weak or Evade-related). Will take commissions for custom light armor components.

---

**Working arrangement**  
All three pay the temple for hearth rights. Master Harran can recommend one of them if asked, but he will not mediate disputes over price or personality. The artisans set their own rates and keep their own ledgers.

**Tension locked: Master Harran vs. Lady Elara**

**Core conflict**  
Harran holds that the working of metal into weapons and armor is a sacred act, not a commercial one. The heat, the hammer, the quench, and the final edge are offerings as much as they are products. Because of this, he refuses to let the Temple of the Forge fall under the Traders’ Hall or any commercial licensing structure controlled by Lady Elara.

Elara sees an entire category of high-value goods moving through the city with only light formal oversight. She wants the Forge’s output registered, taxed, and brought under the same marketplace rules that govern every other stall. To her it is simply incomplete regulation.

**How it shows up**

- Forge-made mechanical gear (Tier 1 and above) is sold at the temple itself or through the independent artisans under its roof. It does **not** appear on the open Marketplace boards or under Traders’ Hall licenses.
- Elara’s Wardens occasionally “inspect” the temple precinct. They are received with cold courtesy and minimal cooperation.
- Harran will not sell to intermediaries who intend to resell under a commercial license. Buyers must come to the temple.
- Quiet pressure exists on both sides: Elara can make certain metal shipments and fuel allotments slower; Harran can make certain high-quality commissions simply unavailable to people known to be close to the Fox.

**Current balance**  
Neither has forced a final confrontation. The temple still operates. The Fox still watches. The independent artisans (Brannok, Calda, Vesper) live in the space between them — they pay the temple for hearth rights and therefore inherit Harran’s protection, but they also feel Elara’s commercial gravity whenever they try to move larger quantities of work.

The disagreement is religious on one side and administrative on the other. Both believe they are defending the proper order of the city.